well now the uh leader of the brexit  party nigel farage has expressed  skepticism about that brexit deal  negotiated by the prime minister just  announced by the prime minister  mr price says the deal would keep the uk  far too closely aligned with the  european union the former member of the  european parliament also  says uh he hopes this would be the  beginning  of the end of the european union  well uh let's hear it from him himself  uh mr fraz joins me now  very good to talk to you and uh happy  christmas to you is this the  the kind of christmas present you wanted  from the prime minister i'm guessing  from what i said there  it's not oh look i'm saying it's not  perfect and i'm worried that we're going  to be too closely aligned to eu rules  and  indeed you know that's what the eu  bosses are saying um that we  will not be able to step out of line uh  without them having the threat of  imposing immediate tariffs but look  that detail we'll discover in the next  couple of days  all right what i did think was good news  was when barney has said  that the british have made no  commitments on foreign policy or defense  that sounded like really quite good  progress  and if we take the big picture you know  and don't forget i've been campaigning  for us to leave for 30 years and this is  may not be perfect but it's a very very  big day  and a big step forward so look overall  of course of course i've got concern  over areas  but is this progress are we far better  off than we were five years ago  absolutely if you're an mp would you  vote for it  uh well i'd need to see the detail and i  would not want to be bounced  into voting for it in a couple of days  having not read it so i think what  parliament should do  is have a vote in principle and then in  the new year  go through it in detail in principle  given where we are right now given that  this has been the most divisive issue in  british politics for three decades  if this brings some peace and some  stability  then yes in principle i would okay uh  but what do you think  i want to ask you about the the prime  minister's language there about the  continuing connections with the european  union uh when he talks i mean  obviously he's used this language an  awful lot our eu partners and friends we  know that  but about being culturally emotionally  historically and strategically  attached to europe and that uh  we remain uh collaborative with uh the  eu over  issues like science and he also said our  basic goals  remain the same yeah i just shook my  head  i don't i don't agree at all i think we  actually have a much closer cultural  affinity  with many parts of the english-speaking  world than we do with continental europe  and  and that's one of the reasons of course  in the end that we voted for brexit  um you know boris does his pc bit um  plays up the pro-european side of things  he of course himself until the  referendum came along  had always been a supporter of the  european project but look  the truth of it is whatever he said  there and you know i'd also question  what he said about the fact  that we've got a canada style deal we  haven't um  you know you have to take what boris  says with a pinch of salt i think the  truth of it is  that the european union itself is  divided from north to south by a  currency  that doesn't work for the south at all  divided east to west culturally are we  seeing  you know the eu budget being vetoed and  countries like poland and hungary  very unhappy and i think in the broader  picture brexit is the beginning of the  end of the european union  i'm pro-european i'm pro-europe of  sovereign states  trading and being friends not being run  from brussels  but why does that matter to you that uh  i mean it still seems the project goes  on there you want to see the  disintegration of the  the european union you know the uk is  out you got what you wanted let the eu  do what the eu's going to do  no i don't agree i spent 20 years over  there um  and i saw the the the undemocratic  nature  of the way this project is run the fact  that it benefits big multinational  businesses  and damages all the small men and women  trying to do their best  and as history teaches us you know when  europe is happy and settled  it makes our lives easier too and all i  can see  is conflict building up north south east  and west with  within a european union uh that frankly  just wants to build this modern day  empire with an absence of democracy so  no it does matter to us  what happened i'm just thinking though  mr farage if you want to start a global  campaign  against tyranny empire building and lack  of democracy is the  the european union the most egregious  example what about the chinese  i couldn't agree more i couldn't agree  more the chinese are far  worse than anything we see in brussels  it's an utterly monstrous regime  um uh it of course uh has brought us  covid  and did its best to cover it up um and  yes  and and i'm worried about that because  you know  boris johnson declares himself to be a  synophile the only western world leader  really standing up to china was donald  trump  and if indeed joe biden comes into the  white house well the chinese communist  party are getting a bit of a free pass  okay this is a wide-ranging interview i  want to get let's get back then  to the deal what prime minister has been  saying about the european union we  haven't got the details as we've been  discussing  uh fully about the deal but what we do  know is uh let me put these two there's  a  another transition period when it comes  to fishing five and a half years  for the eu to adapt and then then  renegotiations over  over the quotas independent third party  arbitration when it comes to disputes  about things like subsidies and  and level playing fields i mean it still  seems like uh  the uk is going to remain pretty much  bound by the eu's rules  well that's the biggest worry i've got  you know we haven't yet seen the detail  on what this arbitration committee is um  i guess they're using that phrase  because we didn't want to hear the ecj  you know was the final arbiter um look  yeah you know  there are two big problems that i have  with this i want to see the detail the  first  you're quite right if we're going to  stay aligned to eu rules  and dare not step out of line because  they have the sword of damocles  hanging over us that if we become too  competitive they'll slap tariffs on well  that would be a huge disappointment  and on fisheries i mean you know to say  to the british industry  it'll be 10 years 10 years  after the country voted brexit before  we're even catching two-thirds of the  fish  in our waters isn't a good deal um  and you know for example i mean next  year  and british boats in devil and cornwall  land bluefin tuna yes or no  i don't know the answers to that i'm  hoping we'll get some detail over the  course of the next few days but the  fishing industry  the coastal communities today are  disappointed northern ireland  today is of course extremely  disappointed  uh those of us who want britain to  become competitive and global  a concern over what those level playing  for  provisions mean and and i share all of  those concerns  but the point i'm making is that on the  31st of january this year  and the first of january 2021 we've made  the big  historic break the brexit wars are over  and there's no going back and i will at  least celebrate that this evening  and and is that the main benefit from  your point of view this the sovereignty  argument the  the emotional side of it the the end of  the the brexit wars the end of the  relationship because a lot of people and  a lot of the debate during  the referendum campaign was about the  economic benefits when  and if do you think they will flow well  you know what when it comes to economics  you can get two experts on a panel one  i'll tell you will be poorer  one will tell you we could be a lot  richer and they both could be wrong  what is beyond doubt is that with brexit  we get back something that we used to  think was rather valuable and it's  called democracy  and it means that you can actually vote  for people that make your laws  and then vote the people who want to get  rid of them and the biggest problem with  the european union  was any single piece of eu law that came  into british law  there was nothing the electors could do  in a general election or a parliament  could do  even if all 650 of them voted against it  we  literally given away the ability to  govern ourselves  and brexit makes us a free country it  makes us a democratic country  it doesn't by the way mean we'll get  everything right  but it does at least mean we'll make our  own successes  and our own failures in the future ah  well i wanted to ask you about that on  the economic front because you know it's  kind of been  polarized into singapore on 10 singapore  on sea  or staying very much like the eu  a bigger role for the state huge  investment in public services and  following rules  and regulations i mean do you tend  towards if i may term it that the  singapore  model cutting the red tape cutting the  taxes encouraging the investment  of course i think you know i mean the  whole idea of the european single market  the whole idea of a level playing field  is  anti-competitive and big state in its  very nature  and it makes it much more difficult for  smaller medium-sized competitors  to break into industries that are run by  a handful of big companies  so for me the economic vision of brexit  well number one it was global  because you know let's face it the  eurozone  is only 15 of the world's gdp and it  goes down one percent every year so the  future  for traders are going to be outside  europe um but i want us to be  on tax on regulation employment  legislation environmental law  i think we can legislate smarter and  better and make this country  far more competitive you know than  germany or france and elsewhere and if  we get that right  then you know the thought of singapore  is that we get money  you know hundreds of billions flooding  into britain  from all over the world and so the  biggest concern  economically is just how closely tied  are we going to be to those european  rules and what's the arbitration process  going to be  and if if we have signed up to something  that stops us from having the option of  being singapore  that will be a great shame okay always  good talking to you mr farage  nigel ferrari thank you very much indeed

